// @check
// @error-at 13:13 cannot infer a type for 'xs' — an empty array literal has no element type until it is used; use it where its type is known, or annotate the binding (`const xs: i32[] = []`)
// @error-at 15:13 cannot infer a type for 'ys' — an empty array literal has no element type until it is used; use it where its type is known, or annotate the binding (`const ys: i32[] = []`)
// @error-at 17:15 cannot infer a type for 'm' — an empty Map has no key or value type until it is used; use it where its type is known, or annotate the binding (`const m: {[string]: i32} = Map()`)
// AN OPERAND WHOSE TYPE IS STILL THE IN-BAND `-1` MUST DIAGNOSE, NOT TRAP THE COMPILER.
// `xs[0]` over an `[]` whose element is unpinned answers `-1` (`arrElemValueTy`'s "no
// answer"), and every arm below the assignment arm of `checkBinExprNodeReal` — plus the
// `is` soundness gate — used to index `T.tys` with it. `T.tys[-1]` is an out-of-bounds
// array access, so the user received a wasm backtrace naming a function number in a
// compiler they did not build, indistinguishable from their own program trapping. D1462.
//
// The element is GENUINELY under-determined here: `xs[0] + 1` is satisfied by `i32`,
// `i64`, `u8`, `f32` and `f64` alike, so the binding's own diagnostic is the answer.
const xs = []
print(xs[0] + 1)
const ys = []
print(ys[0] is i32)
const m = Map()
print(m["a"] ?? 1)
